var e=1,t=`funds-waterfall-01`,n=`fund-structures`,r=`Distribution Waterfalls, Hurdles and Catch-Up`,i=`The order money is paid out in — and how one clause can increase the manager's share of profits by a third.`,a=`advanced`,o=[`funds-fees-01`],s=12,c=[`CAIA-L1`,`CAIA-L2`,`practical`],l=[{type:`concept`,id:`what-it-is`,body:`A fund sells an investment. Cash arrives. Who gets it, and in what order?

The answer is the [[waterfall]], and it is called that because money fills each tier before any spills into the next. A standard four-tier structure:

**Tier 1 — return of capital.** LPs get back what they put in. Nothing else happens until this is complete.

**Tier 2 — [[preferred-return]].** LPs receive a minimum return on that capital, commonly 8% a year, before the GP shares in anything. The threshold itself is the [[hurdle-rate]].

**Tier 3 — [[catch-up|catch-up]].** The GP now receives a high share, often 100%, of further distributions until it has caught up to its full profit share on *all* profits including the preferred return.

**Tier 4 — the split.** Everything remaining divides in the agreed ratio, typically 80% LP and 20% GP.

The first two tiers are widely understood. **Tier 3 is where the money is**, and it is the one most often skimmed.`},{type:`example`,id:`worked-waterfall`,body:`A fund called $100m and returns $180m in total. The preferred return accrued is $20m. Carry is 20% with a 100% catch-up.`,walkthrough:[`**Total profit** = $180m - $100m = **$80m**. Hold that number: the GP's target is 20% of it.`,`**Tier 1.** LPs receive $100m — their capital back. Remaining: $80m.`,`**Tier 2.** LPs receive the $20m preferred return. Remaining: **$60m**.`,`**Tier 3 — the catch-up.** The GP's full entitlement is 20% of *total* profit = 20% x $80m = **$16m**. It has received nothing so far, so it takes $16m from the $60m pool. Remaining: $44m.`,`**Tier 4.** The $44m splits 80/20... except the GP has already reached its 20% of total profit, so in this example the catch-up consumed the whole entitlement and the remainder goes to LPs.`,`**Result: LPs receive $164m, the GP receives $16m.** Check it: $16m / $80m profit = exactly 20%. The catch-up did its job — it delivered the GP its full 20% of everything.`,`**Now remove the catch-up.** With a *hard* hurdle, the GP earns 20% only of profits above the preferred return: 20% x $60m = **$12m**. LPs receive $168m.`,`**So the catch-up clause is worth $4m to the GP — 33% more carry — on identical fund performance.** Same deals, same exits, same $180m. One clause.`]},{type:`intuition`,id:`soft-versus-hard`,body:`That $4m is the whole reason to understand this properly, and the distinction has a name.

**A soft hurdle (with catch-up)** means the preferred return is a *timing* device, not a giveaway. Once the fund clears 8%, the GP catches up and ends with 20% of everything — including the first 8%. The LP got their preferred return earlier, not permanently.

**A hard hurdle (no catch-up)** means the preferred return is genuinely carved out. The GP never earns carry on the first 8%, so its effective share of total profit is permanently below 20%.

In the worked example: 20% versus 15% of total profit. **The GP's economics differ by a third and both funds can describe themselves as "8% hurdle, 20% carry".** Both descriptions are accurate. Neither is complete.

So the question "what is the carry?" is insufficient. The complete question is: **what is the carry, is the hurdle hard or soft, what is the catch-up rate, and is the preferred return compounding?** Four numbers, and the headline figure is only one of them.`},{type:`concept`,id:`european-vs-american`,body:`A second, equally consequential distinction: **is the waterfall applied to the whole fund, or deal by deal?**

**[[european-waterfall|European, or whole-fund]].** No carry is paid until LPs have received all their capital plus the preferred return across the *entire* fund. The GP waits, sometimes many years, and the LP's downside protection is strong because the netting happens before any carry is paid.

**[[american-waterfall|American, or deal-by-deal]].** Carry is paid on each successful exit as it happens, without waiting for the rest of the portfolio. The GP is paid much earlier — which is the point — and the LP now carries a real risk: **carry paid on early winners may turn out to be unjustified once the losers are realised.**

That risk is why deal-by-deal structures include a [[clawback]]: an obligation to return excess carry at the end of the fund's life. And a clawback is only as good as its enforceability. Ask whether it is joint and several across the GP's individuals, whether it is net of the tax the recipients already paid, whether any of it is escrowed, and whether the entity that owes it will still exist in eight years. **An unsecured, tax-adjusted clawback against a dissolved entity is a sentence in a document rather than a protection.**`},{type:`table`,id:`the-four-questions`,caption:`The terms that determine what carry actually costs`,headers:[`Term`,`LP-friendly`,`Why it matters`],rows:[[`Hurdle type`,`Hard (no catch-up)`,`The difference between the GP earning 20% and 15% of total profit — a third of its carry.`],[`Preferred return basis`,`Compounding`,`8% simple over five years is 40%; compounded it is 47%. On $100m that is $7m.`],[`Waterfall timing`,`[[european-waterfall|Whole-fund]]`,`Netting happens before any carry is paid, so no clawback is needed.`],[`[[catch-up|catch-up]] rate`,`Lower (50% rather than 100%)`,`Slows the GP's catch-up, leaving more with LPs for longer.`],[`[[clawback]]`,`Escrowed, joint and several, gross of tax`,`Determines whether the protection exists in practice or only on paper.`],[`Carry percentage`,`Lower`,`The headline number, and usually the least negotiable — which is why the others matter.`]]},{type:`concept`,id:`how-it-goes-wrong`,body:`**Assuming the headline terms describe the economics.** "2 and 20 with an 8% hurdle" is compatible with the GP taking 20% or 15% of profits. The clause that decides it is one line in a long document.

**Deal-by-deal carry with a weak clawback.** The GP is paid on winners early; the losers arrive later; and the mechanism to recover the overpayment is unfunded, reduced for tax, and owed by individuals. This is the single most consequential term-level risk in private funds.

**Simple rather than compounding preferred return.** Over an eight-year fund this is a large number quietly favouring the GP, and it is easy to miss because both versions are described as "8%".

**Interim distributions before capital is returned.** Some structures allow the GP to take carry on distributions before LP capital is fully repaid. That is the deal-by-deal problem in a more aggressive form.

**Fee income excluded from the profit calculation.** If transaction fees are not offset, they are GP income the LP funded, sitting outside the waterfall entirely.

**Carry crystallised on unrealised value.** Rare in closed-end funds and worth checking for, particularly in hybrid and evergreen structures: paying carry on marks means paying on the GP's own valuation of assets nobody has bought.`},{type:`pitfall`,id:`model-it`,body:`**Do not read a waterfall — model it.** The clauses interact, and their combined effect is not obvious from reading them in sequence. A catch-up rate, a hurdle type and a compounding basis together can move the LP's net outcome by several percentage points a year, and no amount of careful reading substitutes for running the numbers.

The practical method: take three fund outcomes — poor (1.2x), middling (1.7x), strong (2.5x) — and compute LP net proceeds under the actual terms and under a plain vanilla alternative. The difference at each outcome is what the terms cost, expressed in money. That is a table you can put in front of an investment committee, and it makes a document argument into a numbers argument.`},{type:`onTheJob`,id:`on-the-job`,body:`**Ask the four questions together: hurdle hard or soft, catch-up rate, compounding or simple, whole-fund or deal-by-deal.** Any single answer is uninformative. All four determine the economics.

**If carry is deal-by-deal, interrogate the clawback properly.** Is any of it escrowed, and how much? Is it joint and several among the individuals, or owed only by an entity? Is it computed gross or net of tax already paid? Each answer changes whether the protection is real.

**Model the terms at three outcomes and express the difference in money.** "These terms cost us 1.4 points a year at a 1.7x outcome" is a sentence a committee can act on. "The catch-up is 100%" is not.

**Check whether the preferred return accrues on committed or drawn capital.** On committed is more LP-favourable and less common.

**Ask when the GP last agreed a change to its standard terms, and to whom.** It tells you both what is negotiable and where you sit in the queue.

**Look for carry on unrealised value.** In any structure that is not a classic closed-end fund, establish explicitly whether carry can be paid on marks.`},{type:`keyTakeaways`,id:`takeaways`,items:[`Money fills each tier before spilling into the next: capital, preferred return, catch-up, then the split. Tier 3 is where the money is.`,`A 100% catch-up gives the GP 20% of all profit; a hard hurdle gives it 20% above the preferred return only. In the worked example that is $16m against $12m — a third more carry, identical performance.`,`Both structures can honestly be described as "8% hurdle, 20% carry". The headline terms do not determine the economics.`,`Deal-by-deal carry shifts real risk to the LP, and the clawback that offsets it is worth only as much as its escrow and enforceability.`]}],u=[{id:`funds-waterfall-01-q1`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:2,tags:[`waterfall`,`tiers`,`definition`],concept:`what-it-is`,stem:`In a standard fund waterfall, in what order is cash distributed?`,choices:[`Carried interest, then return of capital, then the preferred return`,`Return of capital, then the preferred return, then the catch-up, then the residual profit split`,`The preferred return, then return of capital, then carried interest`,`The residual profit split first, with the earlier tiers settled at the end of the fund's life`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`This inverts the structure. The GP's share sits last in the sequence, and that priority is the central protection the waterfall gives LPs.`,`Correct. Each tier fills completely before the next receives anything: capital back, then the preferred return, then the catch-up that brings the GP to its full percentage, then the ongoing split.`,`A tempting order, because the preferred return is described as the first slice of profit. It is — but profit only exists once capital has been returned, so return of capital comes ahead of it.`,`The residual split is the final tier, not the first. It is what remains once the earlier tiers have been satisfied.`],explanation:`The sequence is worth memorising because every negotiated variation — hard versus soft hurdle, deal-by-deal versus whole-fund, the catch-up rate — is a modification to one tier of this same ladder. Learn the ladder once and you only have to learn what moved.`},{id:`funds-waterfall-01-q2`,type:`numeric`,difficulty:3,tags:[`waterfall`,`carry`,`catch-up`],concept:`worked-waterfall`,stem:`A fund called $100m and returns $180m. The preferred return accrued is $20m. Carry is 20% with a 100% catch-up. How much carry does the GP receive, in millions?`,answer:16,tolerance:.5,toleranceType:`abs`,unit:`$m`,inputHint:`Answer in millions of dollars.`,explanation:`Total profit is $80m, and the catch-up delivers the GP 20% of all of it: $16m. LPs receive $164m. The catch-up exists precisely so the preferred return does not permanently reduce the GP's share.`},{id:`funds-waterfall-01-q3`,type:`numeric`,difficulty:4,tags:[`waterfall`,`hard-hurdle`,`carry`],concept:`soft-versus-hard`,stem:`Same fund, same $180m — but the hurdle is HARD, with no catch-up. How much carry does the GP receive, in millions?`,answer:12,tolerance:.5,toleranceType:`abs`,unit:`$m`,inputHint:`Answer in millions of dollars.`,explanation:`With a hard hurdle the GP earns 20% only of profits above the preferred return: 20% x ($180m - $100m - $20m) = 20% x $60m = $12m. Its share of total profit is 15%, not 20% — and both funds can describe themselves as "8% hurdle, 20% carry".`},{id:`funds-waterfall-01-q4`,type:`numeric`,difficulty:4,tags:[`waterfall`,`catch-up`,`economics`],concept:`soft-versus-hard`,stem:`By what percentage does the catch-up clause increase the GP's carry in this example?`,answer:33.3,tolerance:1,toleranceType:`abs`,unit:`%`,inputHint:`Answer in percent, to 1 decimal place.`,explanation:`($16m - $12m) / $12m = 33.3%. One clause, a third more carry, on identical fund performance. This is why "what is the carry?" is an incomplete question.`},{id:`funds-waterfall-01-q5`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:3,tags:[`waterfall`,`hurdle`,`definition`],concept:`soft-versus-hard`,stem:`What does a soft hurdle with a full catch-up mean for the preferred return?`,choices:[`The LP keeps the preferred return permanently, outside the profit split`,`The preferred return is a timing device — the LP receives it earlier, but the GP catches up and ends with its full share of all profit`,`The preferred return is only paid if the fund exceeds its target return`,`The preferred return is paid to the GP rather than the LP`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`That describes a hard hurdle, where the preferred return is genuinely carved out of the GP's carry base.`,`Correct. The LP is paid first, which has real value in timing and in downside cases. But once the fund performs, the catch-up restores the GP to 20% of everything including that first tranche.`,`The preferred return accrues on LP capital and is paid before carry; it is not contingent on exceeding a separate target.`,`The preferred return is an LP entitlement by definition.`],explanation:`The distinction only bites when the fund performs well. In a weak fund a soft hurdle behaves much like a hard one, because there is never enough to catch up with — which is why the difference is easy to overlook until it is expensive.`},{id:`funds-waterfall-01-q6`,type:`tfj`,difficulty:4,tags:[`waterfall`,`clawback`,`deal-by-deal`],concept:`european-vs-american`,stem:`"Deal-by-deal carry is fine because the clawback protects us." True or false — and why?`,isTrue:!1,justifications:[`False — a clawback is only worth its enforceability: whether it is escrowed, joint and several, and gross of tax already paid`,`False — clawback provisions are unenforceable in every jurisdiction`,`True — a clawback fully reverses any excess carry, so the structures are economically equivalent`,`False — because deal-by-deal carry is prohibited in institutional funds`],justificationIndex:0,rationales:[`Correct. The obligation is typically owed by individuals who have already paid tax on the money and spent some of it, years after the fact, possibly through an entity that no longer exists. Escrow, joint and several liability, and a gross-of-tax calculation are what turn it from a sentence into a protection.`,`Too strong. They are generally enforceable in principle — the practical difficulty is collection, not validity.`,`This is the claim under test. Even a perfectly enforced clawback leaves the LP without the use of the money in the interim, and perfect enforcement is not the norm.`,`Deal-by-deal carry is common in institutional funds, particularly in the US. It is negotiated, not prohibited.`],explanation:`The distinction to hold onto: a whole-fund waterfall makes the netting happen *before* carry is paid, so no recovery is ever needed. A deal-by-deal waterfall pays first and relies on recovery afterwards — and recovery from individuals years later is a genuinely different proposition.`},{id:`funds-waterfall-01-q7`,type:`numeric`,difficulty:3,tags:[`waterfall`,`preferred-return`,`compounding`],concept:`the-four-questions`,stem:`An 8% preferred return on $100m over 5 years. How much larger is the accrued preferred return if it compounds rather than being simple, in millions?`,answer:6.93,tolerance:.3,toleranceType:`abs`,unit:`$m`,inputHint:`Answer in millions of dollars, to 1 or 2 decimal places.`,explanation:`Simple: 8% x 5 x $100m = $40m. Compounded: $100m x (1.08^5 - 1) = $46.93m. A difference of $6.93m that goes to LPs before any carry — and both are described as "8% preferred".`},{id:`funds-waterfall-01-q8`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`waterfall`,`diligence`,`on-the-job`],concept:`model-it`,stem:`You want to show an investment committee what a fund's waterfall terms actually cost. What is the most useful way to present it?`,choices:[`A summary of the clauses with the LP-unfriendly ones highlighted`,`LP net proceeds under the actual terms versus vanilla terms, at three fund outcomes, with the difference in money`,`A benchmarking table of the terms against market standard`,`The GP's own illustrative waterfall model`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`A clause summary describes the document without quantifying it. A committee cannot weigh "100% catch-up" against anything.`,`Correct. Three outcomes — poor, middling, strong — with the money difference at each turns a legal discussion into a numbers discussion, and shows where the terms bite hardest.`,`Benchmarking is useful context and does not tell you what the terms cost this fund at this expected outcome.`,`The GP's model will be accurate and will be built around a favourable scenario. Build your own, and run the poor outcome.`],explanation:`The clauses interact, so their combined effect is not readable from reading them in order. Modelling at three outcomes also reveals something a single case hides: terms that look modest in a strong fund can be expensive in a middling one.`}],d=!1,f=null,p={schemaVersion:1,id:t,domain:n,title:r,summary:i,level:a,prereqs:o,estMinutes:12,examRelevance:c,lesson:l,questions:u,needsReview:!1,reviewNote:null};export{p as default,n as domain,s as estMinutes,c as examRelevance,t as id,l as lesson,a as level,d as needsReview,o as prereqs,u as questions,f as reviewNote,e as schemaVersion,i as summary,r as title};
